designs.summary:
Replace the 2-3-launch sliding-verify path with ONE cluster launch per step, called swf. The grid is B*C CTAs, with a cluster of C (16/8/4 by band) per request and 256 threads, i.e. two warpgroups (one per kv head) as today. Each CTA owns a fixed, contiguous range of 64-key tiles counted from the window's END: 2, 4 or 8 tiles, the same per-CTA load as today's stream-K (528/132 = 4.0 and 1056/132 = 8.0).

While the seqlen -> page-table -> KV gather chain is in flight, each CTA computes its request's q (RMSNorm, RoPE, e4m3). Rank 0 also computes the 8 new K/V rows, stores them to the cache and keeps them as a compute-only smem tile. The main loop runs the board's wgmma tile body unchanged. The C partials are then merged through DSMEM in fixed rank order, with the norm-gate epilogue fused.

What it removes:
- the pre-attention node (2.9 / 2.8 us marginal at B=16/32);
- the combine node (2.6-3.1 us marginal);
- the stream-K prefix and ticket machinery, and every global workspace.

Why it is not the refuted tk merge:
- partials never leave shared memory;
- no atomics, __threadfence or L2 round trips;
- every rank merges 1/C of the rows behind one hardware cluster barrier;
- the slot count is fixed at C instead of growing with the grid;
- the board's own sliding_cluster_kernel already runs this exact merge inside a 9.6 us kernel.

Expected judge-net times, verify 1 / 16 / 32 sliding: 13.9 / 19.5 / 27.1 -> about 10.2 / 14.4 / 22.4 us. Headline 1.634 -> about 1.71 (x1.046 central; x1.022 low, x1.068 high). A decode port follows: it replaces SKW at B=1 and the pre-attention + FA3 + norm-gate path at B>=2, worth +0.4-0.8% on decode.

Two prepare-only rungs tonight de-risk the schedule claims:
- T1: B=1 anatomy of today's window kernel.
- T2: an swf scaffold that measures the DSMEM merge, the chain and the fold, and checks the new prologue and merge numerics on the GPU.

