spec.summary:
Winner: design 1 (swf), corrected. Design 2 (fvs) keeps only its zero-new-CUDA diagnostic, which runs tonight, and a conditional stage 4.

Today's sliding verify path:
- B=1: 2 graph nodes (pre-attention, then the PSEUDO stream-K kernel with an in-kernel ticket merge).
- B=2..32: 3 nodes (pre-attention, slidewin_verify_wgmma_kernel<SPLIT>, PDL sw_combine_kernel).

swf makes each step ONE cluster launch:
- grid B*C, cluster C = 16/8/4 by band, 256 threads (one warpgroup per kv head), ~111.7 KB smem, 2 CTAs/SM;
- each CTA owns a fixed, tail-aligned slice of the request's 32 old 64-key tiles (2/4/8); rank 0 also holds the 8 new keys as an smem tile;
- the main loop is the board's wgmma tile body, copied unchanged;
- the C partials merge over DSMEM in fixed rank order with the norm-gate fused (the reduce-scatter sliding_cluster_kernel already runs on the board);
- no workspace, ticket, fence or atomic.

Two arms per band, raced in prepare against kernel_fn's real incumbent:
- PRE=1 folds pre-attention into the prologue AFTER every gather is issued: q RMS/RoPE/e4m3 into the wgmma A-fragment words, and rank 0's new K/V into the cache and the smem tile.
- PRE=0 keeps today's pre-attention node and reads its q8 and new rows.
PRE=0 is new. Today's sv1 FUSED fold into the B=1 PSEUDO kernel LOST on the judge: 16.4 vs 15.3 us, 0.934x (7284659f). Its fold sat on the critical path: new rows built between the page-index request and the tile issue, and q rebuilt per segment at consume time.

Judge's numbers (corrected formula, board 4e386647 rows):
- central: verify 1/16/32 sliding 13.90/19.51/27.10 -> 10.8/15.0/23.0 us, x1.039 (design claimed x1.046 at 10.2/14.4/22.4);
- PRE=0 only: x1.024; low x1.018; high x1.056;
- the stage-1 check should read ~1.67 from base X's ~1.623 level;
- the decode port that follows: +0.6-0.9% on decode.

fvs (design 2: full verify B=1 off FA3 on the window kernel at window 0) is built only if tonight's free rung shows that kernel streams 80k keys in <= 14 us. The board's tile body costs ~2 us per tile per SM: B=32 kernel alone 22.8 us at grid 132, 21.2 at grid 264. 1251 tiles therefore project to ~20-24 us, against FA3's 14.8 us main kernel. Design 2's x1.035 rests on a c320 (q_len 1) analogy that does not hold.

Neither design reaches FA3's full verify at B=16/32 (25.6% of the headline). That needs a faster main loop, a separate lever.

spec.floor:
TASK.md / reference.speed_of_light_ns, at 4.8 TB/s and 989 TFLOP/s.

verify 1 sliding:
- tokens = 1 x 2048 x 1.0 = 2048;
- bytes = 2048 x 2 x 64 x 2 = 524,288, plus 1 x 8 x 36 x 64 x 2 x 2 = 73,728, total 598,016 B -> 0.125 us;
- FLOPs = 4 x 2048 x 8 x 16 x 64 = 67.1 M -> 0.07 us;
- floor 0.12 us.

verify 16 sliding:
- tokens = 16 x 2048 x 0.925 = 30,310 -> 7.76 MB, plus 1.18 MB = 8.94 MB -> 1.86 us.

verify 32 sliding: 60,621 tokens -> 17.88 MB -> 3.72 us.

verify 1 full: 80,000 x 256 + 73,728 = 20.55 MB -> 4.28 us.

Practical floor of the one-launch structure:
- one graph node: about 1.0-1.15 us judge;
- three dependent cold round trips (seqlen -> page index -> KV), which the board's K=8 kernel puts at about 5.5 us device including one tile and the store;
- the KV stream: 0.53 / 8.4 / 16.8 MB. With today's tile body at about 2 us per tile per SM (about 1.1 TB/s equivalent) that adds about 0.5 / 7.6 / 15 us; a 3 TB/s body would add 0.2 / 2.8 / 5.6;
- a merge of at least about 0.5 us.

So the floor is about 7 / 14 / 22 us with today's body (swf's targets of 10.8 / 15.0 / 23.0 sit near it), and about 7 / 10 / 13 with a 3 TB/s body. The leaders' 8-12 us rows at B=16/32 need that faster body, which is the main-loop lever.

